Re-examining my earlier answer, I made an error. The ship that laid the first permanently successful transatlantic telegraph cable in 1866 was the **SS Great Eastern** — Isambard Kingdom Brunel's massive vessel, converted for cable-laying duty. The Great Eastern also attempted the 1865 laying, which failed when the cable snapped, and it was chartered by the Atlantic Telegraph Company under the direction of engineer Willoughby Smith, with Cyrus Field again leading the project overall. In July 1866 it successfully completed the cable from Valentia Island, Ireland, to Heart's Content, Newfoundland, and later that year it also recovered and completed the lost 1865 cable, giving two working lines.

The "Great Western" was a different Brunel ship entirely (an 1838 wooden paddle steamer used for transatlantic passenger service), and it had no role in the cable project. My earlier answer was incorrect.

